1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,920
They laughed when you said
you would sell a spreadsheet.

2
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:06,130
Your boss. Your ex. Your uncle.

3
00:00:06,230 --> 00:00:10,060
Eighteen months from now, every one
of them will ask you for something.

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:12,960
But tonight, you have forty-three dollars.

5
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:15,700
And in nine hours, you are
about to lose your job.

6
00:00:18,550 --> 00:00:22,772
You are twenty-six years old, and you
deliver parcels for a company that does

7
00:00:22,772 --> 00:00:23,814
not know your name.

8
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:29,213
To them, you are a number on a
badge. Driver four four seven one.

9
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:34,970
Ten hours a day. Six days a week. Two
hundred stops before the sun goes down.

10
00:00:35,510 --> 00:00:39,149
Your van is older than your career,
and it sounds like it knows it.

11
00:00:39,585 --> 00:00:41,369
Every month ends the same way.

12
00:00:41,369 --> 00:00:44,580
The money runs out about a
week before the month does.

13
00:00:44,962 --> 00:00:48,797
You live in a room so small you can
touch both walls at the same time.

14
00:00:49,233 --> 00:00:51,842
There is a leak in the
ceiling. You have named it.

15
00:00:51,842 --> 00:00:53,669
That is how long it has been there.

16
00:00:54,121 --> 00:00:55,940
Maya left four months ago.

17
00:00:56,222 --> 00:01:00,617
She did not shout. She just said she was
tired of waiting for your life to start.

18
00:01:00,944 --> 00:01:04,953
And the worst part is, you could not
think of a single thing to say back.

19
00:01:05,304 --> 00:01:09,591
Every Sunday, at dinner, Uncle
Ray asks you the same question.

20
00:01:09,591 --> 00:01:11,251
"Still driving the van?"

21
00:01:11,538 --> 00:01:16,103
Everybody laughs. You laugh too,
because it is easier than not laughing.

22
00:01:16,556 --> 00:01:21,138
Your mom squeezes your hand under the
table. Somehow, that makes it worse.

23
00:01:21,374 --> 00:01:25,252
At work, Mr. Graves times your
bathroom breaks with a stopwatch.

24
00:01:25,490 --> 00:01:29,017
"Four minutes, four four seven one.
That is coming out of your lunch."

25
00:01:29,201 --> 00:01:31,791
You tell yourself this is temporary.

26
00:01:31,791 --> 00:01:35,460
You have been telling
yourself that for five years.

27
00:01:35,605 --> 00:01:39,991
And every night, you fall asleep hoping
that tomorrow, somehow, will be different.

28
00:01:41,191 --> 00:01:43,384
That night, you cannot sleep.

29
00:01:43,649 --> 00:01:47,777
So you do what you always do. You
pick up your phone, and you scroll.

30
00:01:48,195 --> 00:01:54,948
A cat. A car crash. A man dancing in
a kitchen. Nothing. Nothing. Nothing.

31
00:01:55,483 --> 00:01:59,783
And then a stranger says one sentence
that stops your thumb in mid-air.

32
00:02:00,045 --> 00:02:01,834
"You do not need a factory.

33
00:02:01,834 --> 00:02:06,206
You need one file that solves one
problem, sold a thousand times."

34
00:02:06,466 --> 00:02:11,293
He calls them digital products.
Templates. Guides. Little tools.

35
00:02:11,579 --> 00:02:15,879
You make it once. It costs nothing to
copy. And it sells while you sleep.

36
00:02:16,314 --> 00:02:19,781
For the first time in months,
something in your chest moves.

37
00:02:20,015 --> 00:02:25,014
You watch another video. Then nine more.
Suddenly it is four in the morning.

38
00:02:25,411 --> 00:02:28,072
And then you remember who you are.

39
00:02:29,372 --> 00:02:33,301
You are a van driver. What could
you possibly sell to anybody?

40
00:02:33,497 --> 00:02:38,964
Still, the idea will not leave you alone.
So at lunch, you mention it to Dex.

41
00:02:39,103 --> 00:02:41,839
Dex laughs so hard he drops his sandwich.

42
00:02:41,889 --> 00:02:46,098
"Bro. You? Selling files
on the internet? To who?"

43
00:02:46,213 --> 00:02:48,952
You tell your mom. She goes very quiet.

44
00:02:49,269 --> 00:02:53,544
"Honey, those things are scams.
Please. Just keep the job."

45
00:02:53,794 --> 00:02:58,547
Uncle Ray does not even look up from
his plate. "Real men have real jobs."

46
00:02:58,705 --> 00:03:02,684
Nobody is being cruel. They honestly
believe they are protecting you.

47
00:03:02,923 --> 00:03:05,500
But the loudest voice
is not any of theirs.

48
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:07,596
It is the one inside your own head.

49
00:03:07,899 --> 00:03:10,845
It will never work. Not
for someone like you.

50
00:03:11,112 --> 00:03:16,096
So you delete the videos. You set
your alarm. And you go back to work.

51
00:03:16,796 --> 00:03:19,971
And that is exactly when
the real problem arrives.

52
00:03:20,227 --> 00:03:24,034
Monday morning, Mr. Graves calls
every driver to the loading bay.

53
00:03:24,435 --> 00:03:28,266
The company has bought a new routing
system. It only needs half of you.

54
00:03:28,624 --> 00:03:32,991
He reads the badge numbers out loud,
one at a time, like a shopping list.

55
00:03:33,281 --> 00:03:34,706
Four four seven one.

56
00:03:35,651 --> 00:03:38,799
"Nothing personal. You were never
going to be more than this anyway."

57
00:03:39,029 --> 00:03:43,648
You carry your things out in a cardboard
box. The same kind you used to deliver.

58
00:03:43,961 --> 00:03:46,821
When you get home, there is
a notice taped to your door.

59
00:03:47,155 --> 00:03:51,206
Forty-three dollars. No job.
Thirty days to pay the rent.

60
00:03:51,431 --> 00:03:54,456
You sit down on the floor, with
your back against the door.

61
00:03:54,719 --> 00:03:56,895
And you realise something strange.

62
00:03:56,895 --> 00:04:00,928
For the first time in your life,
you have nothing left to lose.

63
00:04:02,628 --> 00:04:04,063
You find the videos again.

64
00:04:04,332 --> 00:04:09,439
But this time, you do not just watch them.
You study them, with a pen in your hand.

65
00:04:09,834 --> 00:04:14,380
The library has free internet and a
heater. So you basically move in.

66
00:04:14,555 --> 00:04:19,594
You learn the first rule. Do not
sell what you like. Sell what hurts.

67
00:04:19,718 --> 00:04:23,643
So you ask yourself a different
question. Who do you actually know?

68
00:04:23,643 --> 00:04:24,814
And what hurts them?

69
00:04:25,159 --> 00:04:28,656
Drivers. Forty of them just
lost their jobs with you.

70
00:04:28,656 --> 00:04:30,965
Thousands more work for themselves.

71
00:04:31,101 --> 00:04:36,410
And every single one of them hates the
same three things. Tracking miles.

72
00:04:36,410 --> 00:04:38,082
Tracking fuel. And tax.

73
00:04:38,232 --> 00:04:42,783
You know that pain. You tracked your own
numbers in a notebook for five years.

74
00:04:42,970 --> 00:04:46,825
So what if that notebook was a simple
spreadsheet that any driver could use?

75
00:04:46,993 --> 00:04:52,288
You give it a name. The Driver's Ledger.
And you give it a price. Nine dollars.

76
00:04:52,498 --> 00:04:55,363
For the first time, the
idea is not a dream.

77
00:04:55,363 --> 00:04:58,685
It is a file, on your screen,
with your name on it.

78
00:04:59,095 --> 00:05:03,903
It is not much. But it is the first thing
you have ever made that belongs to you.

79
00:05:04,603 --> 00:05:06,503
You build it in six nights.

80
00:05:06,605 --> 00:05:11,777
You have no idea how to make a sales
page. You make one anyway. It is ugly.

81
00:05:12,134 --> 00:05:15,678
And then, with your heart
pounding, you press publish.

82
00:05:15,967 --> 00:05:17,988
And you wait for the money to roll in.

83
00:05:18,393 --> 00:05:22,482
Day one. Nothing. Day five. Nothing.

84
00:05:22,775 --> 00:05:26,063
Day twelve. One visitor. It is your mom.

85
00:05:26,350 --> 00:05:30,145
Dex sends you a screenshot of your
page, with three laughing faces.

86
00:05:30,227 --> 00:05:34,802
The rent counter says eighteen days.
Your balance says eleven dollars.

87
00:05:35,018 --> 00:05:40,502
Everything inside you says stop. And this
is the exact moment where most people do.

88
00:05:40,738 --> 00:05:42,580
Your finger is on the delete button.

89
00:05:42,892 --> 00:05:48,923
But instead, you ask the one question that
changes everything. Why is nobody buying?

90
00:05:50,423 --> 00:05:53,761
So you stop guessing. And you go and ask.

91
00:05:54,039 --> 00:05:58,169
You stand in the cold outside the depot,
and you talk to every driver who will give

92
00:05:58,169 --> 00:05:58,815
you a minute.

93
00:05:59,039 --> 00:06:04,067
And they tell you the truth. It is too
complicated. They want it on their phone.

94
00:06:04,067 --> 00:06:04,821
In two taps.

95
00:06:05,178 --> 00:06:10,081
It hurts to hear. But it is the most
valuable thing anybody has ever told you.

96
00:06:10,685 --> 00:06:13,805
So you tear the whole thing
down, and you build it again.

97
00:06:14,202 --> 00:06:17,057
Only one person offers to help. Sam.

98
00:06:17,401 --> 00:06:20,799
He tests it on his own route,
and he breaks it eleven times.

99
00:06:20,895 --> 00:06:22,898
So you fix it eleven times.

100
00:06:23,150 --> 00:06:27,622
You post a thirty second video showing
how it works. It gets forty views.

101
00:06:27,769 --> 00:06:30,029
You post another one. And another.

102
00:06:30,029 --> 00:06:33,287
Every single day, whether
anybody watches or not.

103
00:06:33,577 --> 00:06:38,045
Forty views. Sixty. Thirty-one.
The numbers do not move.

104
00:06:38,045 --> 00:06:39,801
You keep going anyway.

105
00:06:40,228 --> 00:06:41,121
Day forty-six.

106
00:06:41,121 --> 00:06:46,093
You sell your guitar, the last thing you
own worth selling, to cover the rent.

107
00:06:46,143 --> 00:06:50,593
Day forty-seven. You wake up to a
sound you have never heard before.

108
00:06:50,869 --> 00:06:55,887
Two fourteen in the morning. Somebody you
have never met just paid you nine dollars.

109
00:06:56,925 --> 00:06:58,702
Nine dollars. It is nothing.

110
00:06:58,702 --> 00:07:02,953
And it is the first money you have
ever made while you were asleep.

111
00:07:03,755 --> 00:07:06,005
By the end of the week,
there are six more.

112
00:07:06,232 --> 00:07:10,508
Then a driver with fifty thousand
followers shares your video.

113
00:07:10,508 --> 00:07:13,543
He writes four words.
"This saved my month."

114
00:07:13,899 --> 00:07:16,839
The sales counter does
not climb. It jumps.

115
00:07:17,024 --> 00:07:19,917
And then, you make your
first real mistake.

116
00:07:19,917 --> 00:07:23,011
A bug wipes the data of
two hundred customers.

117
00:07:23,077 --> 00:07:28,290
You could hide. Instead, you email every
single one of them. You refund them.

118
00:07:28,290 --> 00:07:30,185
And you fix it in one night.

119
00:07:30,279 --> 00:07:34,201
Most of them buy again. And some of
them tell their friends exactly why.

120
00:07:34,431 --> 00:07:36,653
You add a second product. Then a third.

121
00:07:36,653 --> 00:07:39,958
Every one of them built from
something a driver asked for.

122
00:07:40,140 --> 00:07:43,522
Nine dollars at a time, the
numbers stop looking like a hobby.

123
00:07:43,522 --> 00:07:45,431
They start looking like a business.

124
00:07:45,558 --> 00:07:50,254
You stop being the man who got fired.
You become the man who builds things.

125
00:07:50,954 --> 00:07:55,536
And one morning, you notice something.
You did not check your bank balance.

126
00:07:55,715 --> 00:08:00,663
Eighteen months after the loading bay,
sixty thousand drivers use something that

127
00:08:00,663 --> 00:08:01,220
you made.

128
00:08:01,397 --> 00:08:03,382
The grey hoodie goes into a drawer.

129
00:08:03,537 --> 00:08:07,809
You move somewhere with windows.
Somewhere you cannot touch both walls.

130
00:08:08,119 --> 00:08:12,725
For a while, you just stand in the middle
of the room, and listen to how quiet it

131
00:08:12,725 --> 00:08:12,896
is.

132
00:08:13,161 --> 00:08:15,272
The first person you hire is Sam.

133
00:08:15,484 --> 00:08:18,751
And you pay off the house your mom
has been renting for thirty years.

134
00:08:19,061 --> 00:08:23,284
She does not say anything. She
just holds the key, and cries.

135
00:08:24,984 --> 00:08:28,017
And then, one by one, they come back.

136
00:08:28,238 --> 00:08:29,478
Dex wants a job.

137
00:08:29,749 --> 00:08:33,387
Uncle Ray wants a loan. At
dinner, he calls you sir.

138
00:08:33,583 --> 00:08:37,318
Maya sends one message. "I
always knew you would make it."

139
00:08:37,632 --> 00:08:39,448
And then there is Mr. Graves.

140
00:08:39,448 --> 00:08:43,267
His depot was shut down by the
same system that replaced you.

141
00:08:43,594 --> 00:08:47,951
He applies for a job at your company. He
does not recognise the name on the door.

142
00:08:48,238 --> 00:08:52,686
He walks into the interview, looks up,
and his glasses slide down his nose.

143
00:08:53,466 --> 00:08:55,453
You could laugh. You do not.

144
00:08:55,742 --> 00:08:59,981
You just slide a card across the
table. On it, there is a number.

145
00:08:59,981 --> 00:09:01,286
Four four seven one.

146
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:05,099
And underneath it, two
words. Nothing personal.

147
00:09:07,399 --> 00:09:11,855
The best revenge was never the suit.
It was never the look on his face.

148
00:09:12,115 --> 00:09:16,538
It was the nine dollars. The proof
that the voice in your head was wrong.

149
00:09:16,894 --> 00:09:18,542
You do not need a big idea.

150
00:09:18,542 --> 00:09:22,874
You need the notebook you already
have, and the nerve to press publish.

151
00:09:23,152 --> 00:09:25,141
So. What is your notebook?

152
00:09:26,545 --> 00:09:30,585
Subscribe. Because next week,
somebody else starts from zero.
